  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 321 PM EDT Wed May 27 2026\n\n...Friday - Sunday...\n\nQuite an interesting upper pattern through the weekend as central \nCONUS omega block is bookended by anomalous closed lows over the \nwestern and eastern CONUS. For the most part, we'll be under the \ninfluence of the upper ridge, and surface high pressure centered \naround the Great Lakes region. This will keep us mostly dry, however \nwe'll have to focus on southern KY as a stalled boundary just to the \nsouth could provide focus for scattered diurnally driven shower and \nstorm potential each day. Will continue with isolated to widely \nscattered shower and storm chances down across southern KY, however \nthe rest of southern Indiana and central Kentucky are expected to \nstay dry through this stretch.\n\nLREF probabilities along the southern KY/TN border for measurable \nrainfall are ranging in the 30-50 percent range each day, however \nprobabilities for above .25\" of rain each day drop off to 10% or \nless. So, not expecting large amounts of rainfall at the moment.\nTemperatures during this stretch should be pretty near normal values \nfor this time of year in the upper 70s and low 80s.\n\n...Sunday Night - Wednesday...\n\nThe upper ridge axis will retrograde west early next week as \nreinforcing shortwaves dig into the eastern CONUS trough. This \nprogression will help to push the aforementioned surface boundary \nfarther to the south and out of play. In addition, surface high \npressure builds in over the area as well. Dry conditions will \ncontinue with near normal temperatures in the upper 70s and low 80s. \nIt is also worth noting that dew points will likely stay in the low \nto mid 50s next week. By mid week, the upper ridge axis slides back \ntoward our region as the eastern CONUS height anomaly slides off the \nNew England coast. Dry conditions continue.",
